SafePal disclosed a data breach affecting 39,798 customers after a flaw in its order-tracking system was exploited to steal customer information. The breach impacts customers who placed orders between March 2, 2025, and April 11, 2026. Exposed data includes names, email addresses, shipping addresses, phone numbers, and purchase information. SafePal states that wallet seed phrases, private keys, passwords, bank details, payment card numbers, and government IDs were not compromised.

A threat actor is selling the stolen data on a cybercrime forum. SafePal customers have reported phishing emails and phone calls as early as May, suggesting the data has been in active use for months before the company's August 16 notification. SafePal launched an online verification tool letting customers check whether their order details were stolen.

For a cryptocurrency hardware wallet company, the exposure of customer names and shipping addresses is significant even without wallet credentials. It enables targeted phishing campaigns against people who have publicly associated themselves with crypto holdings. The three-month gap between earliest phishing reports and SafePal's notification raises questions about detection timelines. Customers should treat any SafePal-branded communication received since May as potentially fraudulent until independently verified.
